==========================================
NETWORKING DAY 11 — TCPDUMP PART 2
Filters: host, port, protocol
==========================================

--- LOCAL TEST SERVER (safe traffic source) ---
python3 -m http.server 8080              # Terminal 1: tiny web server on your own machine
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8080/ > /dev/null   # Terminal 3: generate a request to capture

--- FILTER BY PORT ---
sudo tcpdump -i lo -n port 8080           # traffic on port 8080 (either direction)
sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -n port 53            # DNS traffic
sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -n src port 443        # packets coming FROM port 443
sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -n dst port 80          # packets going TO port 80

--- FILTER BY PROTOCOL ---
sudo tcpdump -i lo -n tcp                  # TCP only
sudo tcpdump -i lo -n udp                   # UDP only
sudo tcpdump -i lo -n icmp                   # ICMP only (ping)

--- FILTER BY HOST AND DIRECTION ---
sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -n host 1.1.1.1          # to OR from that host
sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -n dst host 1.1.1.1       # only packets going TO it
sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -n src host 1.1.1.1        # only packets coming FROM it
sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -n net 192.168.80.0/24      # a whole subnet

--- COMBINING FILTERS ---
sudo tcpdump -i lo -n 'tcp and port 8080'          # AND: both must match
sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -n 'icmp or port 53'           # OR: either matches
sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -n 'not port 22'                # NOT: exclude (hides your own SSH session)
sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -n 'host 1.1.1.1 and not icmp'   # mix them freely
   # tip: wrap multi-word filters in quotes so the shell doesn't misread them

--- SAVE WITH A FILTER, READ IT BACK ---
sudo tcpdump -i lo -n port 8080 -c 12 -w http8080.pcap     # capture only matching packets to a file
tcpdump -r http8080.pcap -n                                  # read it back
tcpdump -r http8080.pcap -n 'tcp[tcpflags] & tcp-syn != 0'    # filter the SAVED file: only SYN packets

--- READING TCP FLAGS IN THE OUTPUT ---
[S]    SYN             connection request
[S.]   SYN-ACK          connection accepted
[.]    ACK               acknowledgement
[P.]   PSH-ACK            data being pushed (e.g. an HTTP request/response)
[F.]   FIN-ACK             connection closing
[R]    RST                 connection reset/refused

